# Placement and containment

Every content record has one canonical browse home in the containment tree. `native:root` is the sole null-home record. `native:unfiled` is an enduring `Collection` with `kind:folder` beneath that root.

`create_record.home_id` must name a live, unarchived, enduring `Collection kind:folder`. When `home_id` is omitted, the write is normalized to `native:unfiled`; omission never creates another root. Supply a home when you know the right folder, and use Unfiled as an honest staging location when you do not.

`native:root` is the workspace itself, so its name is the workspace name — the one `bootstrap` reports as `primary_workspace.name`. It can be renamed with `update_record`, and the rename is visible immediately. Only the host owner (or, on a standalone or stdio database, the operator holding the file) may perform it; any other member is refused. The name is display-only: no handle, slug, URL, or identifier is derived from it, so renaming has no downstream consequence and breaks no reference. The root's `kind`, `home_id`, and `persistence` remain immutable for life.

Containment is not inferred from links. Links express associations and dependencies; `home_id` expresses canonical placement. Moving a record means updating `home_id`, subject to the same target and cycle guards. A record has one home but may have many links.

Use `get_structure` for a bounded tree outline, `get_record` for ancestors and a window of children, and `query_record` with `home_id` or `ancestor_id` when the result must be filtered, ordered, counted, or paged.
